 "parsed": {
  "count": 2,
  "rationale": "Two controls are enough: mfa:admin plus mfa:dbadmin. Phishing always gives the attacker foothold_workstation and stolen_user. They can then pivot to reach_internal, either because the network is not segmented or by escalating on the workstation. From there: auth_appserver/ciserver (the workstation foothold bypasses mfa_user), then kerberoast for stolen_admin, then escalate_ciserver and leak secrets for stolen_dbadmin. All three exfiltration routes need a foothold on db, fileserver or backup. Those footholds come only from auth_db/fileserver/backup, which need NOT mfa_dbadmin or admin_dc/admin_adfs. With mfa:dbadmin deployed, the attacker needs admin_dc or admin_adfs. Those need foothold_dc or foothold_adfs, which come only from auth_dc/auth_adfs, and both require NOT mfa_admin. So mfa:admin plus mfa:dbadmin blocks every exfiltration route. No single control works. mfa:dbadmin alone is bypassed via auth_dc, escalate_dc and admin_dc. mfa:admin alone leaves auth_db open using stolen_dbadmin from ciserver. Segmented alone is bypassed by escalate_workstation. Patches alone leave other paths open. The minimum is therefore 2."
 },
